  },
  {
    "@id": "https://www.saskatoonsymphony.org/temporary/c7efc3cd-8c24-4abd-8696-b8da17c6be8b",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://saskatoonsymphony.org/event/accent-with-the-sso-2/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\n\t\t\t\n\n\n7:30PM, Saturday, March 26, 2022\nTCU Place, Sid Buckwold Theatre\n\n\n35 – 22nd Street East\nSaskatoon, SK S7K 0C8\nGoogle Maps Directions\n\n\nPurchase In-Person Tickets\n\n\n\n \nMartin MacDonald, conductor\nAccent\nThis concert is made possible thanks to the generosity of Dr. Anne Neufeld.\nWe’re thrilled to finally have Accent joining us on stage – after waiting two years since we had to cancel their performance due to the pandemic.\nThis concert is special as Accent is doing their first full orchestral Pops show in the hometown of two of its members! Danny Fong and Andrew Kesler will surely feel the roar of the crowd to welcome these two successful Saskatoon artists home again.\nIn the increasingly popular world of a cappella singing groups, Accent has carved out its own niche. Inspired by the wizardly jazz harmonies of TAKE 6 and, before them, The Hi-Lo’s, the six men of Accent, all of them accomplished arrangers and instrumentalists in their own right, are taking their love of close-harmony jazz singing – the denser and more intricate the better – as far as it can go.\nThe improbable meeting of Jean-Baptiste (“JB”) Craipeau (France), Simon Åkesson (Sweden), Danny Fong (Canada), Andrew Kesler (Canada), James Rose (United Kingdom), and Evan Sanders (USA), six self-described “jazz vocal nerds,” could have happened only in the age of social media. After becoming long-distance fans of each other’s multi-tracked videos on YouTube, they joined forces in 2011, collaborating online on songs and videos.\nSince then, they have released five albums, performed at the London A Cappella Festival, played dates across Europe, North America and Asia, and were featured in a sold-out Christmas show at London’s Royal Albert Hall. Their singular blend and arrangements have been acclaimed by a cappella devotees and fellow musicians alike, including Cedric Dent of TAKE 6 and Clark Burroughs of The Hi-Lo’s.\n[box]Proof of vaccination will be required at the door, along with a valid photo ID. Alternatively, if you are not vaccinated we ask for proof of a recent negative PCR test or TCU staff will witness a Rapid Test (provided) prior to entry. We ask that all patrons remain masked unless actively eating or drinking. Click for More Info[/box]\n\n \nThe Program\nAccent\nMartin MacDonald, conductor\nAccent\nSaskatoon Symphony Orchestra\nThis concert is made possible thanks to the generosity of Dr. Anne Neufeld.\nUptown Funk\nBruno Mars, J. Bhasker, P. Lawrence, D. Gallaspy, M. Ronson, N. Williams, L. Simmons, R. Wilson, C. Wilson, R. Taylor, and R. Wilson\nThis Will Be\nChuck Jackson and Marvin Yancy\nSugar\nAdam Levine, M. Posner, Dr. Luke, J.K. Hindlin, Ammo, and Cirkut\nAir on a ‘G’ – String\nJ.S. Bach, arr. Simon Åkesson\nRude\nN.T. Atweh, A.D. Messinger, M.R. Pellizzer, B. Spivak, and A. Tanasijczuk\nKi Lo Ta’azov\nLudwig Epstein\nMy Sugar is So Refined\nSylvia Dee and Sidney Lippman\nSong For Gene\nSimon Åkesson and Petter Sandström\nWho You Are\nSimon Åkesson and Evan Seth Sanders\n~Intermission~\nChicago Medley\nJohn Kander\nMarrakesh Express\nGraham Nash, arr. Simon Åkesson\nJust Wondering\nJames Michael Rose\nWith a Little Help from My Friends\nJohn Winston Lennon and Paul James McCartney\nSwe-Danes Symphony\nSvend Asmussen, Ulrik Neumann, and Alice Babs\nHymne à l’amour\nÉdith Piaf and Margueritte Angele Monnot\nKeep the Faith\nGlen Ballard, Siedah Garrett, and Michael Joe Jackson\n \n \nAccent\nhttps://accentvocal.com/\n[box]\nIn the increasingly popular world of a cappella singing groups, Accent has carved out its own niche. Inspired by the wizardly jazz harmonies of TAKE 6 and, before them, The Hi-Lo’s, the six men of Accent, all of them accomplished arrangers and instrumentalists in their own right, are taking their love of close-harmony jazz singing – the denser and more intricate the better – as far as it can go.\nThe improbable meeting of Jean-Baptiste (“JB”) Craipeau (France), Simon Åkesson (Sweden), Danny Fong (Canada), Andrew Kesler (Canada), James Rose (United Kingdom), and Evan Sanders (USA), six self-described “jazz vocal nerds,” could have happened only in the age of social media. After becoming long-distance fans of each other’s multi-tracked videos on YouTube, they joined forces in 2011, collaborating online on songs and videos.\nSince then, they have released five albums, performed at the London A Cappella Festival, played dates across Europe, North America and Asia, and were featured in a sold-out Christmas show at London’s Royal Albert Hall. Their singular blend and arrangements have been acclaimed by a cappella devotees and fellow musicians alike, including Cedric Dent of TAKE 6 and Clark Burroughs of The Hi-Lo’s.\nRead more about Accent.\n [/box]\n \nJean-Baptiste Craipeau — Tenor 1 — France\nSimon Åkesson — Tenor 2 — Sweden\nDanny Fong — Tenor 3 — Canada\nAndrew Kesler — Tenor 4 — Canada\nJames Rose — Baritone — England\nEvan Sanders — Bass — USA\n\nClick here to read about your Orchestra!\nThis concert also includes:\n\nKim de Laforest – violin\nDean McNeill – trumpet\nDavid Fong – piano\nKyle Krysa – drums\n\n\nClick here to learn more about our Partners.\n \n\n\n\t\t"
      }
    ]
  },
